Name :
================
Root Guard

Why we did this
================
BPDU Guard protects access ports from rogue switches.

However, on legitimate switch-to-switch links, BPDUs are expected.

An attacker or misconfigured switch connected to the network may advertise a better Bridge Priority and attempt to become the Root Bridge.

If this happens:

- STP topology changes
- Traffic paths change
- Network performance degrades
- Traffic may be redirected
- Network instability may occur

Administrators wanted to ensure that specific switches always remain the Root Bridge.

To solve this problem, Cisco introduced Root Guard.

Root Guard prevents a neighboring switch from becoming the Root Bridge.

What will happen
================
1. Root Guard is enabled on selected ports.
2. Port operates normally.
3. If the neighbor advertises a superior BPDU:
   - Root Guard detects it.
   - Port enters Root-Inconsistent State.
   - Traffic on that port is blocked.
4. Existing Root Bridge remains unchanged.
5. Once superior BPDUs stop:
   - Port automatically recovers.

Example

Normal Operation
----------------

Core Switch
(Root Bridge)
      |
      |
Distribution Switch

Result:
--------
Stable STP Topology


