Port enters Root-Inconsistent State

Root Bridge Protected

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Protects Root Bridge election.
2. Prevents STP topology manipulation.
3. Automatically recovers after violation removal.
4. Maintains network stability.
5. Stops unauthorized switches from becoming Root Bridge.
6. Recommended on designated downstream ports.
7. Improves Layer 2 resilience.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Incorrect placement may block legitimate switches.
2. Requires STP design planning.
3. Protects only Root Bridge election.
4. Does not detect unidirectional link failures.
5. Additional STP security features may still be required.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even with Root Guard enabled, a unidirectional link failure can still cause incorrect STP calculations.

Example:

Switch A receives BPDUs from Switch B

But

Switch B cannot receive BPDUs from Switch A

This can create unexpected Layer 2 loops.

To protect against this condition, Cisco introduced:

Loop Guard

which prevents ports from incorrectly transitioning to the forwarding state when BPDUs unexpectedly stop.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Configure Root Guard on Downstream Ports

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# spanning-tree guard root

OR

Switch(config)# interface range g0/1-2
Switch(config-if-range)# spanning-tree guard root

Verification
============

show spanning-tree inconsistentports

show spanning-tree interface g0/1 detail

show spanning-tree summary

